   "sha": "fc0a9e94576224c89cc08d390b38ab760a261f1f"
  },
  "body": "Fixes microsoft/monaco-editor#4796 — lines jump up and down while typing Chinese, Safari only.\r\n\r\nSafari and Firefox have no `EditContext`, so input goes through the hidden textarea. During IME composition it is a one-row overlay holding a full page of text, so each render scrolls it to the caret's row (`scrollTop = nlBefore * lineHeight`). WebKit then scrolls that same element itself to keep the caret visible, and stops 3px short of row-aligned because the caret box is shorter than the line box; the next render writes the aligned value back. Trace in the issue: 102 self-scrolls over three composed lines, every one off by exactly 3px.\r\n\r\nIt only holds a page because `getScreenReaderContent` treats `AccessibilitySupport.Unknown` as \"possibly a screen reader\", while `writeNativeTextAreaContent` already treats it as \"none\" and skips render-time writes (#192278). This aligns them — `Unknown` now gets the short content `Disabled` already produces, with no wrap sizing — so nothing overflows and the browser has nothing to scroll. `Enabled` is untouched; Android's `Unknown` still reaches the word branch composition diffing needs. The second commit moves the composition branch's `scrollTop`/`scrollLeft` writes after `_doRender` to match the sibling branch: consistency, not the fix.\r\n\r\nVerification: this change built into Monaco in the app that reproduces the bug (macOS 26, WKWebView, system Chinese IME) — composition no longer jitters on any line, and ASCII typing, select-all, copy/paste, undo and cross-line cursor movement are unchanged. Not verified: Firefox, and screen-reader behaviour under `Unknown`, which now behaves like the everyday `Disabled` path.\r\n\r\n",
  "rebaseable": true
 },
